Lichen Planus (Mild Cutaneous)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Violaceous, flat-topped papules localized to wrists or ankles, resolving with hyperpigmentation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-022
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, intractable pruritus leading to significant excoriation and sleep disruption.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden, highly pruritic rash on the extremities. Prompt clinical differentiation from an acute contact dermatitis, scabies infestation, or an early anaphylactic cutaneous reaction was required to safely manage symptoms and prevent severe secondary soft tissue infection prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: These purple bumps on my wrists have been itching so badly I've scratched my skin raw, and I haven't slept in two days.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an eruption of self-limiting itchy papules on extremities beginning 3 days ago. Tonight, the pruritus became intensely sharp and unrelenting, causing severe distress and psychological exhaustion. The patient has been scratching aggressively, creating open, excoriated wounds. Patient fears a contagious parasitic infection. Denies new soaps, detergents, oral medications, or respiratory symptoms.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely fatigued.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, high risk of localized skin infection due to scratching.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/78 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, visibly distressed, continually rubbing and scratching the wrists.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals violaceous, flat-topped papules localized to wrists or ankles, resolving with hyperpigmentation in older lesions. Fine white lines (Wickham striae) are visible on the surface of the papules. Several areas are acutely excoriated with pinpoint bleeding, but no spreading erythema, warmth, or purulent exudate to suggest secondary cellulitis. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are entirely normal.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment, no evidence of household pests or obvious allergens.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lichen Planus (Mild Cutaneous) - Status: Exacerbated Pruritus
Differentials:

Scabies - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of characteristic interdigital web burrows and the distinct violaceous, flat-topped morphology of the lesions.

Acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Well-demarcated individual papules rather than a confluent, blistering, eczematous plaque, and negative exposure history.

Secondary Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of surrounding induration, spreading erythema, localized heat, or fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe, unremitting pruritus and a spreading rash, causing marked acute distress and raising concern for an infectious or severe allergic etiology. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to rule out scabies or an acute contact reaction. Detailed dermatological assessment identified classic clinical signs of cutaneous Lichen Planus, firmly excluding a transmissible parasite or acute bacterial cellulitis. Immediate intervention with potent topical therapy and oral antihistamines was essential to break the severe scratch-itch cycle and allow the patient to sleep, justifying urgent after-hours care to prevent further self-induced skin injury.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-potency topical corticosteroid ointment (e.g., betamethasone dipropionate) and an oral sedating antihistamine (promethazine) for nocturnal use.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply the steroid ointment directly to the purple bumps and immediately put on long-sleeved cotton shirts before bed. By creating a physical barrier over the medicated skin, you will prevent unconscious scratching in your sleep, allowing the steroid to drastically reduce the inflammation by morning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 7-10 days for review of topical therapy efficacy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to a GP clinic or Emergency Department if the scratched areas become excessively hot, rapidly swollen, or begin draining yellow pus, indicating a bacterial infection.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal dermatological review or biopsy; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This is a localized autoimmune skin condition. The intense itch can be controlled with strong creams, though the spots may take months to fade completely into flat, dark marks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the rash spreads into your mouth or genitals, or if you develop systemic symptoms like fevers and chills.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is mistakenly attacking the top layer of skin cells, causing intense inflammation and itch. It is not an allergy or an infection.
Actionable Steps: Do not wash the area with hot water or harsh soaps. Keep your fingernails trimmed very short to minimize damage when you feel the urge to rub the skin.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Lichen Planus (immune skin rash). Not contagious or an allergy. Strong steroid ointment and nighttime allergy pill prescribed. See GP in 7 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Apply cream and cover with long sleeves to stop scratching at night. Cut nails short. Go to ED if the scratched skin becomes hot, swollen, and infected.
